  "text": "MA\nDesigning a system for 64+ cameras\nPosition\n3 / 9\nLeave learning path\nPath curriculum\n\nReading adds to resources read. For eligibility, select Complete and continue. Your position does not indicate completion.\n\n01\nCalculating camera count and coverage zones\nResource · 9 min\nCompleted\n02\nChoosing a server for 64+ cameras\nResource · 14 min\nCompleted\n03\nCalculating disk capacity for 64 cameras\nResource · 12 min\nCurrent unit\n04\nDistributing recordings across disks\nResource · 6 min\n05\nConfiguring recording retention on DuoStation\nResource · 12 min\n06\nChecking bandwidth before arriving on site\nResource · 8 min\n07\nTRASSIR licensing plan for a project\nResource · 6 min\nFor partners\n08\nEquipment specification for a tender\nResource · 8 min\nFor partners\n09\nLearning path assessment\nAssessment · 30 min\nHardware\n·\nupdated 28 August 2026\n·\n12 minutes\nCalculating disk capacity for 64 cameras\n\nRetention, bitrate and reserve: justify capacity with a calculation, not a guess\n\nThe formula and three adjustments\n\nThe basic calculation is the same as for recording retention. Three adjustments are often forgotten: index overhead, changes in bitrate during motion and free space that must remain available to prevent irregular overwriting.\n\nHow to justify the figure to the customer\n\nBring the calculation and its inputs, not just the total. The customer challenges the lack of transparency, not the capacity: a line reading ‘64 cameras × 4 Mbps × 30 days’ can resolve the disagreement in a minute.\n\nUse the button below to confirm completion for eligibility. Opening the resource alone is not enough.\nBack\nNext · Distributing recordings across disks\nComplete and continue",
